CASES = [
("guss","Victorian Legal Services Board v Guss (Penalty) [2026] VSC 529 &middot; Finanzio J &middot; 20 August 2026",True,[
 ("A Case Study","lg","He is 88. He is going to prison for <em>seven days.</em>","SUB:Over a password he could have handed over in ten minutes."),
 ("The Facts","md","A manager was appointed to run his law practice.","BODY:<p>The court ordered him to give that manager the password to the email account he ran the practice from.</p><p>He refused. Through two judges, and a failed appeal.</p>"),
 ("The Question","md","Was it contempt, and was it <em>criminal?</em>","BODY:<p>Disobeying an order is usually civil contempt.</p><p>It becomes criminal when the defiance is deliberate. The judgment uses the word <b>contumacious.</b></p>"),
 ("The Ruling","md","Conviction recorded. Forty five days.","BODY:<p>Seven to be served, the rest suspended. At the penalty hearing his own IT consultant said compliance would take <b>ten minutes.</b> He handed it over that afternoon.</p>","LEARN:Learn this: an appeal does not stay an order. Until it is set aside, you obey it."),
 ("Why It Matters","md","The court expects <em>more</em> of lawyers, not less.","BODY:<p>His Honour held that his age and experience counted heavily against him, because an officer of the court should understand exactly what an order is.</p>")]),

("bogo","DPP v Bogojevska [2026] VSC 534 &middot; Forbes J &middot; 20 August 2026",False,[
 ("A Case Study","lg","She pleaded guilty to manslaughter. The sentence turned on what came <em>after.</em>","SUB:Supreme Court of Victoria, 20 August 2026."),
 ("The Facts","md","An 85 year old neighbour, and an obstructed airway.","BODY:<p>The offender then hid the body for about a day, stole from the deceased, used her bank card, and left her beside a river.</p>"),
 ("The Question","md","How much does what you do <em>afterwards</em> count?","BODY:<p>The plea accepted the death was not intended. So the assault by itself was never going to set the sentence.</p>"),
 ("The Ruling","md","Eight years. Six non-parole.","BODY:<p>But for the plea of guilty, nine years. She had already served 755 days.</p>","LEARN:Learn this: disposing of a body after an unlawful killing is itself a significant feature of aggravation."),
 ("Why It Matters","md","Sentencing is not a <em>formula.</em>","BODY:<p>Childhood deprivation, kidney failure in custody, an early plea, and no genuine remorse. All of it weighed at once, in a single synthesis.</p>")]),

("rm","RM v DPP [2026] VSC 544 &middot; Croucher J &middot; 21 August 2026",True,[
 ("A Case Study","lg","Ten months in detention. <em>Set aside.</em>","SUB:He was seventeen. He had already served five months."),
 ("The Facts","md","Car thefts. Weapons. A loaded sawn off shotgun.","BODY:<p>The Children&rsquo;s Court sentenced him to ten months detention in a youth justice centre.</p><p>He appealed.</p>"),
 ("The Question","md","Was detention the <em>right</em> sentence?","BODY:<p>A youth appeal is heard completely fresh. And the youth sentencing regime says a court must not impose a heavier order if a lighter one will do.</p>"),
 ("The Ruling","md","Released. No conviction.","BODY:<p>A six month supervision order with conditions for drugs, mental health and offending. Drug charges dismissed. A TAFE place starting next month.</p>","LEARN:Learn this: parsimony is a rule, not a mood. The lightest adequate sentence is the lawful one."),
 ("Why It Matters","md","Rehabilitation is a sentencing purpose, not a <em>soft option.</em>","BODY:<p>His Honour found that more time in custody carried a real risk of undoing the progress he had made.</p>")]),

("marriott","Marriott v Grigorovitch [2026] VSC 535 &middot; Goulden AsJ &middot; 21 August 2026",False,[
 ("A Case Study","lg","She says she was assaulted at a <em>branch meeting.</em>","SUB:Then she sued the members who did not stop it."),
 ("The Facts","md","A political party meeting, October 2023.","BODY:<p>She alleges another member assaulted her. She sued two <b>fellow members</b> in negligence, for failing to protect her.</p>"),
 ("The Question","md","Do members of a club owe each other a <em>duty of care?</em>","BODY:<p>There is no recognised duty between members of an unincorporated association. She had to build one from ordinary negligence principles.</p>"),
 ("The Ruling","md","One claim gone. One survives.","BODY:<p>Against the office holder, no real prospect: he never attended, knew nothing, assumed nothing. Against the other member, a real prospect, but the pleading was struck out and must be redone.</p>","LEARN:Learn this: knowledge and assumption of responsibility are what turn a relationship into a duty."),
 ("Read This Twice","md","Nothing has been <em>decided.</em>","BODY:<p>The assault is alleged. This ruling was only about whether the claims can go forward at all.</p>")]),

("keycon","Keycon Pty Ltd v Modi [2026] VSC 533 &middot; Gray J &middot; 19 August 2026",True,[
 ("A Case Study","lg","He asked for an adjournment at <em>6:06pm</em> the night before.","SUB:It was refused. He appealed. He lost."),
 ("The Facts","md","A builder, and a $112,000 defects claim.","BODY:<p>The director who was meant to run the case could not attend. His son was having surgery that day.</p><p>He emailed the evening before the hearing.</p>"),
 ("The Question","md","Was refusing that adjournment <em>unfair?</em>","BODY:<p>A tribunal must give a party a reasonable opportunity to be heard. Refusing an adjournment can breach that.</p>"),
 ("The Ruling","md","No. Leave to appeal refused.","BODY:<p>The hearing had been listed <b>seven months</b> in advance. No defence filed, no evidence filed. And the company never said what it would have argued if the adjournment had been granted.</p>","LEARN:Learn this: to win on a refused adjournment, show what you would have said, and that it might have changed the result."),
 ("Why It Matters","md","It had a fair opportunity. It just did not <em>take</em> it.","BODY:<p>The court has to give you a reasonable chance to be heard. It does not have to make you ready to use it.</p>")]),

("coolbreeze","Re Cool Breeze Clothing Pty Ltd (No 1) [2026] VSC 530 &middot; Attiwill J &middot; 19 August 2026",False,[
 ("A Case Study","lg","They served the expert report <em>during the trial.</em>","SUB:It did not go well."),
 ("The Facts","md","Liquidators, alleged unfair preferences, and solvency.","BODY:<p>Whether the group could have borrowed money was central. On the first day of the trial, the defendants asked to rely on a <b>new banking expert.</b></p>"),
 ("The Question","md","Can you add an expert <em>that</em> late?","BODY:<p>Only with leave. The court weighs the explanation for the delay, the prejudice to the other side, and the cost to the administration of justice.</p>"),
 ("The Ruling","md","Leave refused.","BODY:<p>The delay was inordinate and inadequately explained. The plaintiffs would have needed their own expert, and the trial would have been adjourned part heard for months.</p>","LEARN:Learn this: prejudice to the other side and to the court&rsquo;s list can outweigh the prejudice to you."),
 ("Why It Matters","md","Case management is not <em>housekeeping.</em>","BODY:<p>Since Aon, a court will let a party lose a point rather than let a timetable collapse. Deadlines decide cases.</p>")]),

("lam","Lam v Leung (Costs) [2026] VSC 540 &middot; Attiwill J &middot; 21 August 2026",True,[
 ("A Case Study","lg","Both sides won. Both sides <em>paid.</em>","SUB:A costs ruling that splits neatly in two."),
 ("The Facts","md","One appeal. Two discovery fights.","BODY:<p>One application sought documents from the other party. A second sought them from a <b>company that was not a party</b> at all.</p>"),
 ("The Question","md","Who pays when each side wins <em>part</em> of it?","BODY:<p>The default is that the costs of an interlocutory application are costs in the proceeding. The court can depart from that.</p>"),
 ("The Ruling","md","Split. One each.","BODY:<p>He pays her costs of the fight he lost. She pays his, and the company&rsquo;s, for the non-party application she abandoned and should not have brought.</p>","LEARN:Learn this: costs follow the event, and the event can be each separate application, not the case as a whole."),
 ("Why It Matters","md","Interlocutory costs are <em>real</em> money.","BODY:<p>Students treat them as a footnote. Clients do not. The side skirmishes are often where the bill is made.</p>")]),

("doran","Doran v Astrazeneca Pty Ltd [2026] VSC 536 &middot; Keogh J &middot; 20 August 2026",False,[
 ("A Case Study","lg","Three class actions. Writs filed, never <em>served.</em>","SUB:And the court let them stay alive."),
 ("The Facts","md","Filed July 2025. Still not served a year later.","BODY:<p>Group proceedings about a class of acid suppressing medications. The writs were filed to <b>protect group members&rsquo; rights</b> while investigations continued.</p>"),
 ("The Question","md","Can a writ be kept alive without <em>serving</em> it?","BODY:<p>A writ is valid for one year. The court can extend it, but only where reasonable efforts have been made to serve, or for other good reason.</p>"),
 ("The Ruling","md","Extended. Another twelve months.","BODY:<p>The application was prompt, the investigations were substantial and ongoing, and several defendants are overseas and must be served under the Hague Convention.</p>","LEARN:Learn this: a writ has a shelf life. Extending it needs a good reason, not just convenience."),
 ("Why It Matters","md","The limitation clock drives <em>everything.</em>","BODY:<p>These writs exist because the deadline was coming. Half of civil litigation strategy is arithmetic about dates.</p>")]),
]
